#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079512} :activity/id #uuid "580243c0-444d-51d9-820f-ab39d2e434fb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049375 :topic/id #uuid "eff38647-ee17-5513-ad67-94525b17bd40" :topic/math-academy-id 621} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079512 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079690 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $y=f(x)$ below. Suppose we know that $f(2)=5$ and $f'(2)=1.$ Based on this information, how can we approximate the value of $f(2.75)?$\n\n![](images/25/25baabc5d4193a480d09370faa104d34edc14b2aa09759f5976d592a387fb398.png)\n\nFirst, recall that the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\ny-f(a) = f'(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$ in the above, we get \n\n$$\ny= f(a)+ f'(a)(x-a)\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $x$ is close to $a,$ we can assume that $y\\approx f(x) ,$ which gives us the approximation\n\n$$\nf(x)\\approx f(a)+ f'(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nWe often represent the tangent line approximation using the notation $L(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\nL(x)= f(a)+ f'(a)(x-a)\n$$\n\nIn this case, we want the tangent line approximation at $a=2.$ Substituting this value along with the given information, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(2)+{f}^{′}(2)(x-2) \\\\  & =(5)+(1)(x-2) \\\\  & =x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the graphs. Notice that in the neighborhood of $x=2$, the function and its tangent line approximation are very close. The line $L(x)$ is a good approximation of the function for points close to $x=2.$\n\n![](images/c1/c1de31c316c019e8faf466f64a0e6ff24a29935a7ec354c2acdf18d3069b8d47.png)\n\nAs we can see, ${\\color{blue}L(2.75)}$ and ${\\color{red}f(2.75)}$ are quite close. So, we can approximate $f(2.75)$ using $L(2.75)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2.75) & ≈L(2.75) \\\\  & =(2.75)+3 \\\\  & =5.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent line approximation\n\n$$\nL(x)= f(a)+ f'(a)(x-a)\n$$\n\nis sometimes also called the **linear approximation** of the function $f(x)$ at the point $x=a.$ The process of approximating the function by its tangent is called **linearization.**" :tutorial/id #uuid "1fd16cbf-e232-56c9-84ba-d6b6ff2d147a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 579 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "80d363a5-ade6-554f-b554-78362a2a4a87" :step/math-academy-id 4825 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079513}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079513 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078566 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078231 :question/id #uuid "aea27069-47b4-50b0-9e70-3a65951916b8" :question/math-academy-id "e-291"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b08650da-89e7-5a9d-89a6-de3623344662" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050644}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319050} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432884}] :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Function at a Point Using a Tangent Line Approximation"} :step/id #uuid "29daedd8-9104-56cf-8960-bc6517ab9536" :step/math-academy-id 4826 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079514}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079514 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078567 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078232 :question/id #uuid "44510fee-857c-5182-8f9e-a08c18966142" :question/math-academy-id "e-292"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b0dd714-5bf1-573f-9ff9-11802622ce92" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048191}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250433} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432886} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432889}] :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Function at a Point Given its Derivative"} :step/id #uuid "0f65d689-6eed-51f9-99e7-de9d4bff5796" :step/math-academy-id 4829 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079515}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079515 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079691 :tutorial/content "Let $L(x)$ be the tangent line approximation of $y = f(x)$ at $x=a,$ as shown in the pictures below. \n\n| ![](images/ad/ad7375c544eed881d96d07b449d9003c7df813bdddcdaf29e82bfda6bd4cb283.png) | ![](images/b5/b5b3cd71da3243a5848021831403adde07f1487ff1077f3359d2564ba0f4b686.png) |\n| --- | --- |\n\nWe can make the following conclusions:\n\n- When the function is *concave up*, the tangent line is *below* the graph (see the picture on the left). Then, \n\n$$\nL(x) \\lt f(x)\n$$\n\nnear $x=a,$ and we say that $L(x)$ gives an *underestimate* of the function.\n\n- When the function is *concave down*, the tangent line is *above* the graph (see the picture on the right). Then, \n\n$$\nL(x) \\gt f(x)\n$$\n\nnear $x=a,$ and we say that $L(x)$ gives an *overestimate* of the function." :tutorial/id #uuid "717ddca8-0e12-5d53-9384-635de0a676ce" :tutorial/math-academy-id 582 :tutorial/title "Underestimate and Overestimate Approximations"} :step/id #uuid "539a8b72-5dd7-5bd3-b6ff-ecd4fb4535ed" :step/math-academy-id 4832 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079516}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079516 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078568 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078236 :question/id #uuid "dc311e80-fd79-5b89-9f0e-0b1caef6457e" :question/math-academy-id "e-293"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f542453-9758-55e6-93c6-6bfd2139ec45" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049011}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297416} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432887}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent Line Approximation and Classifying it as an Underestimate or Overestimate"} :step/id #uuid "0a4e1771-2f14-58d7-9a7d-62eb186154dd" :step/math-academy-id 4833}] :activity/title "Approximating Functions Using Local Linearity and Linearization" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078647}]}
